      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "A static JavaScript scanner flagged this production extension module for possible code obfuscation with high scanner severity but low scanner confidence, and the triggering literal values were not retained. The supplied source contradicts the signal. The module is plainly readable, organized into named functions, and documented with inline comments stating its behavior. Its capabilities are disclosed and proportionate to the extension's stated purpose: it reads the host's already-loaded character array and its metadata, resolves its own installation folder from the browser-provided module URL in order to locate its assets, persists user preferences through the host's settings-save helpers, and requests a character's saved chat filename list from a same-host endpoint only after an explicit user click. Host functions for opening, renaming, and deleting chats are imported and used within visible, user-initiated UI flows. No eval-style execution, encoded payload decoding, hidden string tables, external destinations, or credential handling appear anywhere in the supplied code. The most plausible trigger for the heuristic is the module's large set of benign non-ASCII user-interface text,",
      "layman_explanation": "This appears to be a false alarm from the automated scanner. The scanner guessed that the code might be obfuscated, meaning deliberately scrambled to hide what it really does, but the actual code shown is ordinary, readable, and well-commented. Everything it does matches the extension's advertised purpose: it displays your existing characters, fetches a character's chat list only after you click, and lets you open, rename, or delete chats through SillyTavern's own functions. Nothing in the supplied code hides its behavior, sends data to unexpected places, or touches credentials.",
